  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There will be daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms into at least mid week. Latest mid level water \nvapor imagery shows an upper level trough currently digging \nacross the Ohio River Valley and into the Tennessee River Valley\nthis afternoon and this will be our main weather maker for the \nnext few days. The aforementioned upper trough will eventually\nstall across the Tennessee River Valley on Monday and move\nlittle into midweek. This will keep S to SW'rly flow aloft which\nwill continue to advect in ample moisture from the Gulf and this\nwill also result in several mid level shortwaves transiting\naround the base of this trough and across the Carolinas. The\nfirst shortwave is currently exiting the area this afternoon\nwith the next shortwave forecast to track across the area Mon\nmorning with yet another forecast to track across the area on\nTue. At the surface, closed low in the Midwest will gradually \nshift E'wards into the Ohio River Valley today with its \nassociated surface front stalling just to the west. These \nfeatures, in addition to the sea breeze each afternoon/evening, \nwill be the main forcing present for a notable increase in \nprecip chances. \n\nOngoing shower and thunderstorm activity is forecast to \ngradually decrease in coverage this evening as the\naforementionedshortwave pulls away from the area. Instability \nand shear has not been overly impressive today as MLCAPE values \nhave remained around 1000-2000 J/kg and shear has remained below\n20 kts which has limited storm organization. However, PWATs \nhave shot up to above 2\" as moisture has pooled across ENC as a \nresult of the moisture transport from the Gulf today. With a \ndeep warm cloud layer, and long skinny CAPEs heavy rainfall \nrates have been the norm this afternoon within shower and tstm \nactivity. Luckily storm motion has been rather fast so not \nexpecting any significant hydro issues this afternoon and early \nevening. \n\nHowever, as we get into tonight and Monday, The next in a series\nof shortwaves moves through the area. With slightly better \nforcing, paired with another night/day of long skinny CAPEs and \nPWATs north of 2\" brings yet another threat of heavy rainfall\nand potential for flash flooding, especially east of Hwy 17. A \nFlood Watch remains in effect for coastal NC, starting at 11 PM \ntonight and ending 5 PM Monday. HREF and REFS probs of >1\" of \nrain are over 40% the flood watch region, increasing to 50-70% \nfor Hatteras Island, Ocracoke, Carteret county, and coastal \nOnslow county. Reasonable worst case scenarios show 2-4\" of \nrain in the Flood Watch region, although higher values are \npossible where heavy rain rates linger with slow storm motions. \n\nWe remain in a Slight (level 2/5) Excessive Rain Outlook for \ntonight and into Monday with best chances at seeing flash \nflooding likely along Hwy 17 and points east.\n\nBeyond Monday, the trough/low is still expected to linger to our \nwest until eventually it gets picked up and shifted to our north by \nanother trough moving through the Great Lakes region. As a result, \ndaily elevated precip chances continue through mid-week with a low \nend risk for flash flooding. By the end of the week it looks like we \nreturn back to climo or below climo PoPs as a lack of mid level \nshortwaves or significant forcing will be noted across ENC though we \ndo begin to warm back up with temps returning to the upper 80s to \nlow 90s inland and mid 80s along the OBX.",
